# Application services

Every service takes an explicit actor, authorizes before touching data, and scopes
every query to an organisation.

```ts
// ✅ The canonical shape
export async function archiveProject(input: {
  actor: Actor;
  projectId: ProjectId;
}): Promise<Project> {
  // 1. Authorize first — before any read or write.
  await assertCan(input.actor, "project:archive", { projectId: input.projectId });

  // 2. Load scoped to the actor's organisation, never by ID alone.
  const project = await projects.findById({
    id: input.projectId,
    organizationId: input.actor.organizationId,
  });
  if (project === undefined) throw new NotFoundError("project", input.projectId);

  // 3. Domain rule lives here, not in the transport or the component.
  if (project.status === "archived") throw new ConflictError("Project already archived");

  return projects.archive({ id: project.id, organizationId: input.actor.organizationId });
}
```

## Failure modes this prevents

```ts
// ❌ Reads ambient session state — untestable, and wrong in a job or webhook
const session = await auth.getSession();

// ❌ Authorizes after loading — leaks existence and wastes a query
const project = await projects.findById({ id });
await assertCan(actor, "project:archive", { project });

// ❌ No tenant scope — returns another organisation's row
await projects.findById({ id });
```

Throw typed `AppError` subclasses (`NotFoundError`, `ConflictError`,
`ForbiddenError`). Transports map them to status codes; services never format
HTTP.
